From 6ecd4dd77da20d926fb8f00c193e7eb9b5973c31 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 07:29:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add opt-in disk persistence for uploaded files and the shared
 pad
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Ephemerality stays the default and a feature; persistence is opt-in per
server via config keys persist-files / persist-pad in the server entry,
overridable with COMPANION_PERSIST_FILES / COMPANION_PERSIST_PAD.

Files: blobs are written atomically (tempfile + os.replace) under the
state dir at servers/<name>/files/blobs/<file_id> — UUID names only,
never user filenames — with metadata in files/index.json. Persisted
file content is no longer held in memory: FileEntry grows size and
file_id fields, content becomes Optional, and /download reads the blob
from disk, so the per-client storage cap stops doubling as a RAM cap.
At startup the index is reloaded, entries with missing blobs are
dropped, and orphan blobs (including stale .tmp files from interrupted
writes) are swept.

Pad: mirrored to servers/<name>/pad.json on every update (best effort —
a failed mirror write is logged, not surfaced) and reloaded at startup.
Preview state stays ephemeral: it is a live-session concept.

All writes happen in the single server process under WORKSPACE_LOCK, so
atomic replace is enough — no cross-process file lock like clients.json.

Tests cover blob+index layout, restart survival for files and pad,
storage accounting after reload, orphan sweep, missing-blob drop,
ephemeral default, and env/config flag resolution.
